<section
  id='case-lumastays'
  class='fullscreen-section case-study-section'
  data-title='Case Study: Luma Stays — Web Development | Katja Turnsek'
  data-description='React accommodation booking case study focused on API integration, authentication, routing, booking flows, profile management, and role-based functionality.'
  data-case='lumastays'
  aria-labelledby='case-lumastays-title'
>
  <div class='rows'>
    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-header'>
        <p class='case-eyebrow'>Web Development</p>
        <h1 id='case-lumastays-title'>Luma Stays</h1>
        <p class='case-subtitle'>A complete accommodation booking app for customers and venue
          managers.</p>

        <dl class='case-summary' aria-label='Project summary'>
          <div>
            <dt>Role</dt>
            <dd>UX/UI design and front-end development</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Scope</dt>
            <dd>Individual Project Exam 2</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Year</dt>
            <dd>2026</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Tools</dt>
            <dd>React, JavaScript, Bootstrap, Noroff API</dd>
          </div>
          <div>
            <dt>Outcome</dt>
            <dd>Grade A</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>

        <div class='tech-demo-wrapper case-demo-wrapper'>
          <div class='demo-links' data-auto='demo' data-case='lumastays'></div>
        </div>

        <nav
          class='project-switcher'
          aria-label='Luma Stays sections'
          data-auto='switcher'
          data-case='lumastays'
        ></nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>The brief</h2>
      <p>
        The goal was to turn the Holidaze API into a clear end-to-end booking experience. Customers
        needed to discover venues, understand availability, and manage bookings, while venue
        managers needed a separate set of tools for creating and maintaining listings. The interface
        had to keep these connected journeys understandable without exposing the complexity of the
        underlying API.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='row'>
      <div class='case-study-grid'>
        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--challenge'>
          <h2>Challenge</h2>
          <p>
            The same application had to support two account types, authenticated and public states,
            changing venue data, and booking rules. The main challenge was making the correct action
            obvious at every step while keeping loading, empty, error, and success states stable.
          </p>
          <ul>
            <li>Separate customer and venue-manager journeys without duplicating the whole interface</li>
            <li>Keep venue, profile, and booking data consistent after create and update actions</li>
            <li>Make search and guest filtering useful across real, inconsistent API content</li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--explore'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/site-lumastays-600.webp'
            data-src='site-lumastays-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='484'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Luma Stays explore page showing accommodation cards, search, and filter interface'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Explore stays with search and filtering</figcaption>
        </figure>

        <article class='case-study-block case-study-block--decisions'>
          <h2>Key decisions</h2>
          <ul>
            <li>
              Structured the app as routed React views with reusable components for shared patterns
              such as venue cards, forms, feedback, and protected actions
            </li>
            <li>
              Used role-aware UI states so customers and managers see only the actions relevant to
              their account and the venue they are viewing
            </li>
            <li>
              Added clear validation and feedback around authentication, bookings, profile updates,
              image handling, and venue management
            </li>
            <li>
              Combined Bootstrap structure with custom CSS so the interface stays responsive without
              losing its calm, branded visual style
            </li>
          </ul>
        </article>

        <figure class='case-study-item case-study-media--booking'>
          <img
            class='thumb'
            src='{{base}}assets/images/web-lumastays-booking-600.webp'
            data-src='web-lumastays-booking-600.webp'
            data-path='assets/images'
            width='600'
            height='484'
            loading='lazy'
            decoding='async'
            alt='Luma Stays venue detail page showing booking options and guest selection'
          />
          <figcaption class='case-caption'>Venue details and booking flow</figcaption>
        </figure>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='row case-outcome'>
      <h2 class='case-section-heading'>Outcome and reflection</h2>
      <p>
        The completed project received a
        <strong>Grade A</strong>
        and delivered complete customer and manager journeys in one responsive application. It
        strengthened how I structure larger React projects, recover gracefully from changing API
        data, and turn complex role-based functionality into an interface that still feels calm and
        straightforward.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class='back-to-work-container'>
      <a
        href='{{base}}#work'
        class='label-link back-to-work-label'
        data-back
        data-back-fallback='{{base}}#work'
        aria-label='Back to Work'
      >
        <svg class='icon icon-18 arrow-icon' aria-hidden='true'><use href='#i-arrow-left' /></svg>
        <span class='label-text'>Back to Work</span>
      </a>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>